.SS Setup
.SS Build
.PP
Some dependencies are needed in order to build this software.
The Makefile for GNU/Linux configures the build environment
automatically on Debian and Fedora systems, using their packaging to
install all needed packages.
.PP
The dependencies to be installed on the system for JaroMail are:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
gcc bison flex make autoconf automake sqlite3 libglib2.0-dev libgnome-keyring-dev
\f[R]
.fi
.SS Install
.PP
To install the following programs need to be installed:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
fetchmail msmtp neomutt direnv mblaze pinentry abook wipe notmuch alot
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Then \f[V]make install\f[R] will put all jaromail files in
\f[V]/usr/local/share/jaromail\f[R].
.SS Apple/OSX
.PP
Apple/OSX users that have no experience in building software can obtain
a pre-built universal binary from our download zone on
<http://files.dyne.org/jaromail/binary>
.PP
One can simply drag JaroMail into Applications.
When started JaroMail opens a Terminal window preconfigured with its
environment, to activate it for any terminal add this to
\[ga]\[ti]/.profile\[ga]:
.PP
\f[V]export PATH=/Applications/JaroMail.app/Contents/Resources/jaro/bin:$PATH\f[R]
.SS Use
.PP
Make a directory where all the emails and settings needs to be, change
to the directory and init the environment:
.PP
\f[V]mkdir $HOME/gmail-alternative\f[R]
.PP
\f[V]cd $HOME/gmail-alternative\f[R]
.PP
\f[V]jaro init\f[R]
.PP
Every installation of Jaro Mail is fully reentrant, meaning the
directory where it gets initialised contains all maildirs,
configurations, filters, whitelist, addressbooks and other necessary
files.
.PP
A single user can have multiple Jaro Mail folders on the same system to
permit the complete separation of E-Mail identities.
.PP
If called from outside the installation directory, the \f[B]jaro\f[R]
command will use the environmental variable \f[B]$JAROMAILDIR\f[R] to
find out the active installation being in use.
If one is using a different installation path then should first change
that, i.e:
.PP
\f[V]export JAROMAILDIR=$HOME/OtherIdentities/Luther/Mail\f[R]
.SS Configuration
.PP
The place where Jaro Mail is initialized, its folder contains all
configuration files.
.PP
From now own, we will call this place the \f[B]MAILDIR\f[R].
.PP
Configurations are in readable plain text files that can be edited using
any editor.
Inside them there are comments to explain the settings: all comment
lines start by \[aq]#\[aq] and will be ignored by JaroMail.
.PP
The most important files to start configuring are:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]Accounts/default.txt\f[R] : main account configuration (there can
be more than one file in the \f[V]Accounts/\f[R] folder)
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]Aliases.txt\f[R] : more email addresses one may receive on the
configured accounts
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]Filters.txt\f[R] : Full set of mailinglist sorting rules
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]Applications.txt\f[R] : mime type associations to programs used to
open attachments
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]Mutt.txt\f[R] : mutt specific custom configurations
.SS Accounts
.PP
Inside the Mail directory is found the folder \f[B]Accounts\f[R] with
brief instructions and default templates to fill with Imap and Smtp
account configurations to fetch mail.
A default template will be found in fresh installations:
\f[B]Accounts/default.txt\f[R].
The configuration can be edited with one\[aq]s favourite text editor,
the format of the file is pretty self-explanatory.
.PP
It is possible to have more than one account (simply as a new file in
the Accounts/ directory) and refer to it using the \f[V]-a\f[R]
commandline option.
For instance when we configure \f[V]Accounts/gamer.txt\f[R] as an
alternative mailbox then we can connect it using
\f[V]jaro peek -a gamer\f[R]
.PP
Config files in \f[V]Accounts\f[R] can also contain \f[V]my_hdr\f[R]
that will configure custom header lines (for instance GPG-ID or
X-Organisation) that will be added to the mails sent.
For more information about the vast amount of configurations supported
please refer to the Mutt documentation[1].
.SS Organization
.PP
One of the main goals for Jaro Mail is to organize the e-mail workflow
so that one\[aq]s attention is dedicated to important communications,
rather than being constantly distracted by various degrees of spam and
the need to weed it out of the mailbox.
This ambitious task is pursued by realizing an integrated approach
consisting of flexible whitelisting and the distinction between mails
from known people and the rest.
.SS Folders
.PP
First lets start with a categorization of the standard maildirs and a
brief description for each.
This information is \f[B]very important\f[R] to understand how Jaro Mail
works: these maildirs are standard in Jaro Mail, here they are listed in
order of priority
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
l l.
T{
Folder
T}@T{
What goes in there
T}
_
T{
\f[B]known\f[R]
T}@T{
Mails whose sender is known (Whitelist)
T}
T{
\f[B]priv\f[R]
T}@T{
Unknown sender, we are among explicit recipients
T}
T{
\f[B]unsorted\f[R]
T}@T{
Unknown sender, we are not among recipients
T}
T{
\f[B]zz.blacklist\f[R]
T}@T{
Mails whose sender is not desired (Blacklist)
T}
T{
\f[B]zz.spam\f[R]
T}@T{
Mails that are tagged as spam (server-side)
T}
T{
\f[B]zz.bounces\f[R]
T}@T{
Mail bounces like mailman and similar
T}
.TE
.PP
The advantage using such a folder organization is that every time we
open up the mail reader we will be presented with something we are
likely to be most interested in (known people replying our mails) and
progressively, as we will have the time to scroll through, mails from
\[dq]new people\[dq] or mass mailings of sort.
.PP
This setup is handy especially considering it produces \f[B]sieve\f[R]
filters that can be uploaded to mail servers and processed server-side.
Imagine having your email on a fixed computer, but occasionally checking
it from a mobile phone: server-side filtering will save you time by
presenting a clean INBOX of whitelisted contacts for the mobile phone
use.
.PP
Please note this organization does not includes spam, which is
supposedly weeded out on the server via spamlists: White/Blacklisting
has more to do with our own selection of content sources than with the
generic protection from random pieces of information.
.PP
At last, anything that is matched by filters configured in
\f[B]Filters.txt\f[R] will be saved into the named maildir, whose name
can be freely choosen.
.SS Whitelist
.PP
The way whitelisting works if quite crucial to this setup and, at the
same time, is fairly simple since it does not include any automatic
detection, learning filters, Markov chains or Bayesian A/I.
We believe the user should be in full control of prioritizing
communication channels and at the same time constantly able to tweak the
setup in an easy way.
.PP
To whitelist an address is sufficient to send it an e-mail: at the
moment the message is sent Jaro Mail will remember the destination
address and prioritize all messages coming back from it.
This we call implicit whitelisting.
.PP
To explicitly whitelist an address from inside the mail reader index
press [ \f[B]a\f[R] ] while selecting an email, this will add in the
whitelist the sender address (From: header).
If you want to add all addresses reached by the mail (From: To: and Cc:
fields) use the same letter capitalized pressing shift [ \f[B]A\f[R] ].
.PP
All addresses selected this way will have the privilege of ending up in
your \f[B]known/\f[R] folder, plus their name and e-mail will be
completed automatically when composing a new email, pressing the
\f[B]Tab\f[R] key while indicating them among the recipients.
.SS Blacklist
.PP
To blacklist an address instead one can use the [ \f[B]z\f[R] ] key
while an e-mail is selected on the index: the sender indicated in the
From: field will be downgraded to the very bottom of your priorities,
closes to spam than the rest, the most infamous \f[B]zz.blacklist/\f[R]
folder.
.SS Organization In Brief
.PP
Below a recapitulation of keys related to the white and blacklisting
functionality, to be used in the e-mail index or when an e-mail is open
inside the mail user agent:
.PP
.TS
