.TE
.SS Workflow
.PP
This section goes through a scenario of simple usage for Jaro Mail
.SS Fetch and read your mail at home
.PP
As you acces your computer where Jaro Mail has been configured, you can
open a Terminal and type:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
jaro fetch
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
This will download all new mails.
.PP
If you have configured \f[B]fetchall\f[R] among the imap account
options, then will delete them from the server, freeing online space.
.PP
If you have configured the \f[B]keep\f[R] option, which is the default,
Jaro Mail will only download the email that you have not yet read and in
any case it won\[aq]t delete anything from the server.
Remove the \f[B]keep\f[R] option to delete on the server all emails that
are downloaded.
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
jaro
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
This will launch mutt on the first folder containing unread emails,
starting from the \f[B]known\f[R] folder, then \f[B]priv\f[R], then all
the destinations specified by \f[B]Filters.txt\f[R] exactly in the
ascending order they are listed in that configuration file..
.PP
From there on, pressing \f[B]=\f[R] or \f[B]c\f[R] you can change to
other folders and your \f[B]unsorted\f[R] mails.
.SS Write a new mail
.PP
If you like to write a mail to someone, hit \f[B]m\f[R] and write the
recipient address, you will be then asked about any additional Cc:
recipients.
.PP
If you don\[aq]t remember the emails of the recipients, you can just
type their name or parts of the email you remember, then press the [
\f[B]Tab\f[R] ] key for completion.
A list of addresses may popup with matches found in your whitelist
addressbook to help remind who are you looking for.
.PP
The email is composed using a special Vim (http://www.vim.org/)
configuration that facilitates justifying text to 72 columns using [
\f[B]ctrl-j\f[R] ].
After composing the email you will be able to review it and change:
.IP \[bu] 2
the From: field using [ \f[B]ESC f\f[R] ]
.IP \[bu] 2
the recipient in the To: field using [ \f[B]t\f[R] ]
.IP \[bu] 2
the recipients in the Cc: field using [ \f[B]c\f[R] ]
.IP \[bu] 2
the subject string using [ \f[B]s\f[R] ]
.PP
You\[aq]ll also be able to add more attachments by pressing \f[B]a\f[R]
and use the arrow keys to move over the existing ones and delete them
using [ \f[B]D\f[R] ] (please note that is a uppercase D, because
lowercase d will just add a description for the attachment).
.PP
At last, when ready, pressing \f[B]y\f[R] will queue the email into the
outbox, ready for sending.
.PP
One can review at any time the sending queue, which is just another
maildir named \f[B]outbox\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
jaro outbox
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Mails can be deleted from this view using [ \f[B]d\f[R] ] or edited
using [ \f[B]e\f[R] ] which will allow tweaking of both the header and
body of the email.
.PP
Once sure the outbox contains all what needs to be sent, make sure the
computer is connected to the Internet and issue the \f[B]send\f[R]
command:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
jaro send
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Jaro Mail will send all emails in outbox, one by one, listing their
recipients and size while doing so.
If successful, mails will be removed from the outbox and put into the
\f[B]sent\f[R] folder, which can be accessed from inside mutt or with
the command \f[B]jaro open sent\f[R].
.SS Write a new email from the commandline
.PP
Jaro Mail supports a lot of commandline operations based on stdin/stdout
pipes, which makes it pretty easy to use in scripts that send emails and
attachments.
.PP
If you have written a plain-text email using your favorite editor, you
can send it quickly using the commandline: save the email into a txt
file and then pipe it into \f[B]jaro compose\f[R] followed by a list of
recipients and, optionally a list of filenames to attach.
For example:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
cat Greetings.txt | jaro compose friends\[at]dyne.org picture01.jpg jingle02.mp3 \[ti]/myicons/*
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
The command above may send an email with various separate attachments
(using MIME encapsulation): a picture, an hopefully small audio file and
a list of icons which are all the files contained into the myicons/
directory.
In this case the recipient will be friends\[at]dyne.org, but may be any
other email address found on the commandline in any position.
.PP
Once executed you will find this email in \f[B]jaro outbox\f[R], ready
to be reviewed and sent with \f[B]jaro send\f[R].
.SS Reply messages
.PP
While browsing through the index of emails in various folders, one can
reply any of them just by pressing the [ \f[B]r\f[R] ] key, which will
ask if the original message should be quoted and then open your favorite
editor to compose your text.
.PP
If the email you are replying has been sent to multiple recipients (for
instance using multiple addresses in the Cc: or From: fields) they will
all be included, but you will have the possibility to exclude them by
hand, editing the Cc: field.
To remove them all at once use [ \f[B]ctrl-k\f[R] ] just like deliting a
line on the terminal.
.PP
It is also possible to forward a message to someone else than the
sender, for instance to submit it to his or her attention, or that of a
mailinglist.
To do that, you can use the [ \f[B]f\f[R] ] key which will present you
with the full message and the possibility to write something on top of
it, to describe its contents to its new recipients.
Forwards include all attachments and are sent as attachments themselves,
but this behavious can be changed as a confirmation to \[dq]send forward
as attach\[dq] is asked.
.SS Peek without downloading anything
.PP
If you are around and like to see your new mails without downloading
them, then you can use the \f[B]peek\f[R] function:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
jaro peek
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
This will open the default configured IMAP account and folder over SSL
protocol (securing the data transfer) and allow you to browse, read and
reply your emails without downloading them.
.PP
Using peek you can reply and even delete emails, but be careful since
what you delete here will be removed from the server and won\[aq]t be
there when you download it from home.
.PP
This functionality can be also very useful if you are from a slow
connection and need to delete some email that is clogging it and that
you are not able to download because of its size.
.PP
The peek command will automatically open the INBOX, but also other
remote imap folders can be specified, like for instance \f[B]priv\f[R]
or \f[B]unsorted\f[R] if whitelisting is also setup server-side (the
sieve filters generated by Jaro Mail need to be uploaded on the server).
To have a list of imap folders on the server a command is also
available:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
jaro imap listfolders
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Will list on the terminal all folders found on the imap account, one per
line.
.SS Save important emails for later
.PP
Sometimes one can be on the rush while reading emails (local or via
imap) and flagging them as important can be useful to keep focus on
priorities.
In some cases it is very useful to save such important messages locally
for later reference, for instance in a folder keeping messages that need
to be remembered and that will constitute a kind of TODO list (a\[aq]la
GTD).
.PP
Jaro Mail implements such functionalities: by pressing the [ \f[B]F\f[R]
] key (uppercase) one can flag an email, which will turn bright-green in
the index.
In addition to that there is a folder called \f[B]remember/\f[R] where
one can copy emails on the fly using the [ \f[B]R\f[R] ] key (uppercase)
any time.
Messages will be duplicated into the remember folder (which of course
can be opened with the command \f[B]jaro remember\f[R]) so they can also
be edited with annotations on the task they refer to, for instance using
the [ \f[B]e\f[R] ] key, without affecting the original message.
.SS Workflow in brief
.PP
Below a recapitulation of keys commonly used in our workflow
