.SS Searching
.PP
Searching across all your emails it is as important as demanding of a
task.
Jaro Mail implements it using Notmuch (https://notmuchmail.org/) which
is relying on the Xapian (http://xapian.org) search engine, completely
relying on local computations made on your machine, there is no data at
all being communicated on-line.
.PP
To index and tag all your emails that are locally archived in Jaro Mail
use:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
jaro index
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
This will take a while and increase the size of the storage of about one
sixth of its total occupation, but will definitely come useful when in
need of searching rapidly across all available emails.
To run a search for emails containing the \[aq]\f[I]open
source\f[R]\[aq] string, do
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
jaro search open source
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
To search for all emails containing this string and dated between now
and the last two weeks, do
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
jaro search open source date:2w..
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
The search command prints out a list of found filenames which may be
useful to a script, but less useful to a human.
In order to read a quick summary of the emails found it is possible to
pipe the results into the \f[B]headers\f[R] command which will print out
date, sender and subject of each file
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
jaro search open source date:2w.. | jaro headers
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Searching has also an interactive interface called \f[B]alot\f[R] which
pops up to show search results and browse through them, refine the terms
and in general operate on emails with the usual keys.
One can also reply to emails directly from alot:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
jaro alot search expression strings folder:known
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
To restrict the search to a single folder, one can use the
\f[B]folder:\f[R] prefix to search terms.
Tags can be used also with \f[B]tag:\f[R] as well dates can be specified
with ranges using \f[B]date:\f[R].
Consecutive string expressions are aloud to refine the search match,
connected with logical and/or, plus also the header to search can be
indicated, as for instance \f[B]from:\f[R] or \f[B]to:\f[R].
Read more about this below in the \f[I]Search term\f[R] and \f[I]Date
and time search\f[R] sections (extracts from the
\f[B]notmuch-search-terms\f[R] manpage) and on the notmuch webpage at
<http://notmuchmail.org>
.PP
With the \f[B]addr\f[R] command the search will be run on the whitelist
addressbook entries instead of actual email contents.
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
jaro addr joe
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Will list all addresses matching the string \[aq]joe\[aq] inside the
\f[I]whitelist\f[R] addressbook.
Also the blacklist can be searched this way adding the switch \f[B]-l
blacklist\f[R].
.SS Combining terms
.PP
In addition to individual terms, multiple terms can be combined with
Boolean operators ( \f[B]and\f[R], \f[B]or\f[R], \f[B]not\f[R] , etc.).
Each term in the query will be implicitly connected by a logical AND if
no explicit operator is provided.
.PP
Parentheses can also be used to control the combination of the Boolean
operators, but will have to be protected from interpretation by the
shell, (such as by putting quotation marks around any parenthesized
expression).
.SS Search terms
.PP
The search terms can consist of free-form text (and quoted phrases)
which will match all messages that contain all of the given
terms/phrases in the body, the subject, or any of the sender or
recipient headers.
.PP
As a special case, a search string consisting of exactly a single
asterisk \[dq]*\[dq] will match all messages.
.PP
In addition to free text, the following prefixes can be used to force
terms to match against specific portions of an email, (where <brackets>
indicate user-supplied values):
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
from:<name-or-address>
to:<name-or-address>
subject:<word-or-quoted-phrase>
attachment:<word>
tag:<tag> (or is:<tag>)
id:<message-id>
thread:<thread-id>
folder:<directory-path>
date:<since>..<until>
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
The \f[I]from:\f[R] prefix is used to match the name or address of the
sender of an email message.
.PP
The \f[I]to:\f[R] prefix is used to match the names or addresses of any
recipient of an email message, (whether To, Cc, or Bcc).
.PP
Any term prefixed with \f[I]subject:\f[R] will match only text from the
subject of an email.
Searching for a phrase in the subject is supported by including
quotation marks around the phrase, immediately following
\f[I]subject:\f[R].
.PP
The /<attachment:/> prefix can be used to search for specific filenames
(or extensions) of attachments to email messages.
.PP
For /<tag:/> and \f[I]is:\f[R] valid tag values include \f[I]inbox\f[R]
and \f[I]unread\f[R] by default for new messages added by \f[I]notmuch
new\f[R] as well as any other tag values added manually with
\f[I]notmuch tag\f[R].
.PP
For \f[I]id:\f[R], message ID values are the literal contents of the
Message-ID: header of email messages, but without the \[aq]<\[aq],
\[aq]>\[aq] delimiters.
.PP
The \f[I]thread:\f[R] prefix can be used with the thread ID values that
are generated internally by notmuch (and do not appear in email
messages).
These thread ID values can be seen in the first column of output from
\f[I]notmuch search\f[R]
.PP
The \f[I]folder:\f[R] prefix can be used to search for email message
files that are contained within particular directories within the mail
store.
If the same email message has multiple message files associated with it,
it\[aq]s sufficient for a match that at least one of the files is
contained within a matching directory.
Only the directory components below the top-level mail database path are
available to be searched.
.SS Date and time search
.PP
See \f[I]DATE AND TIME SEARCH\f[R] below for details on the range
expression, and supported syntax for <since> and <until> date and time
expressions.
.PP
The \f[I]date:\f[R] prefix can be used to restrict the results to only
messages within a particular time range (based on the Date: header) with
a range syntax of:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
date:<since>..<until>
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
The syntax \f[I]<initial-timestamp>..<final-timestamp>\f[R] can be
represented using the number of seconds since 1970-01-01 00:00:00 UTC.
.PP
The search syntax also understands a variety of standard and natural
ways of expressing dates and times, both in absolute terms
\[aq]\f[I]2012-10-24\f[R]\[aq] and in relative terms
\[aq]\f[I]yesterday\f[R]\[aq].
Any number of relative terms can be combined \[aq]\f[I]1 hour 25
minutes\f[R]\[aq] and an absolute date/time can be combined with
relative terms to further adjust it.
A non-exhaustive description of the syntax supported for absolute and
relative terms is given below.
.SS The range expression
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
date:<since>..<until>
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
The above expression restricts the results to only messages from <since>
to <until>, based on the Date: header.
.PP
<since> and <until> can describe imprecise times, such as
\[dq]yesterday\[dq].
In this case, <since> is taken as the earliest time it could describe
(the beginning of yesterday) and <until> is taken as the latest time it
could describe (the end of yesterday).
Similarly, date:january..february matches from the beginning of January
to the end of February.
.PP
Currently, we do not support spaces in range expressions.
You can replace the spaces with \[aq]\[rs]_\[aq], or (in most cases)
\[aq]-\[aq], or (in some cases) leave the spaces out altogether.
Examples in this man page use spaces for clarity.
.PP
Open-ended ranges are supported (since Xapian 1.2.1), i.e.\ it\[aq]s
possible to specify date:..<until> or date:<since>..
to not limit the start or end time, respectively.
.PP
Entering date:expr without \[dq]..\[dq] (for example date:yesterday)
won\[aq]t work, as it\[aq]s not interpreted as a range expression at
all.
You can achieve the expected result by duplicating the expr both sides
of \[dq]..\[dq] (for example date:yesterday..yesterday).
.SS Relative date and time
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
[N|number]
   (years|months|weeks|days|hours|hrs|minutes|mins|seconds|secs) [...]
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
All refer to past, can be repeated and will be accumulated.
.PP
Units can be abbreviated to any length, with the otherwise ambiguous
single m being m for minutes and M for months.
.PP
Number can also be written out one, two, \&..., ten, dozen, hundred.
Additionally, the unit may be preceded by \[dq]last\[dq] or
\[dq]this\[dq] (e.g., \[dq]last week\[dq] or \[dq]this month\[dq]).
.PP
When combined with absolute date and time, the relative date and time
specification will be relative from the specified absolute date and
time.
.PP
Examples:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
5M2d
\f[R]
.fi
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
two weeks
\f[R]
.fi
.SS Absolute time formats
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
H[H]:MM[:SS]
[(am|a.m.|pm|p.m.)]
H[H] (am|a.m.|pm|p.m.)
HHMMSS
now
noon
midnight
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Examples:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
17:05
\f[R]
.fi
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
5pm
\f[R]
.fi
.SS Absolute date formats
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
YYYY-MM[-DD]
DD-MM[-[YY]YY]
MM-YYYY
M[M]/D[D][/[YY]YY]
M[M]/YYYY
D[D].M[M][.[YY]YY]
D[D][(st|nd|rd|th)] Mon[thname] [YYYY]
Mon[thname] D[D][(st|nd|rd|th)] [YYYY]
Wee[kday]
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Month names can be abbreviated at three or more characters.
.PP
Weekday names can be abbreviated at three or more characters.
.PP
Examples:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
2012-07-31
\f[R]
.fi
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
31-07-2012
\f[R]
.fi
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
7/31/2012
\f[R]
.fi
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
August 3
\f[R]
.fi
.SS Time zones
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
(+|-)HH:MM
\f[R]
.fi
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
(+|-)HH[MM]
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Some time zone codes.
.PP
Examples:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
UTC
EET
\f[R]
.fi
.SS Compute and visualize statistics
.PP
The \f[B]stats\f[R] command is useful to quickly visualize statistics
regarding folder usage as well the frequency of emails found in a stream
from stdin.
Such streams can be produced by the \f[B]search\f[R] and
\f[B]extract\f[R] commands for instance and passed to stats in order to
have a more graphical (yet ASCII based) visualization of results.
.PP
For example lets visualize the frequency of email domain hosts in our
whitelist:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
jaro addr | jaro stat emails
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Will print out bars and domains in descending order, highlighting the
most frequent email domain in our contacts, which turns out to be very
often gmail.com, unfortunately for our own privacy.
.PP
To visualize the frequency of traffic across our filtered folders in the
past month:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
jaro search date:1w.. | jaro stat folders
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Will show quantities of mails filed to folders during the past week,
quickly highlighting the mailinglists that have seen more recent
activity.
.PP
To see who is most active in a mailinglist which is filtered to a
folder:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
jaro search folder:org.dyne.dng | jaro extract stdin from | jaro stat names
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Will give an overview on who is the most prolific writer in the
\f[I]org.dyne.dng\f[R] mailinglist, filed into the folder by a rule in
\f[B]Filters.txt\f[R] like:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
to    dng\[at]lists.dyne       save         org.dyne.dng
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Please note the \f[B]extract\f[R] command is there to extract email
addresses and names found in the \f[I]From:\f[R] field of all search
hits, the command is explained better in the next chapter: Addressbook*.
.SS Replay results of expensive operations
.PP
Working on the commandline can have some disadvantages.
One of them is that if one runs a long operation to see its result and
forgets to save it also on a file (i.e.\ using tee) the operation needs
to be re-run and saved.
.PP
Jaro Mail helps the user to \f[B]replay\f[R] the last output print by
saving it everytime in its own cache.
Replay can also save per-command outputs so that long pipe chains can be
repeated selectively by naming the command.
Only some commands have the replay capability, to have a list of
available replays on your system do, based on your last run commands:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
jaro replay list
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
To replay the last search command and pipe it into headers to have a
better view of it:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
jaro replay search | jaro headers
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
For instance imagine giving the command that searches for all mails sent
to \f[I]nettime-l\f[R] and extracts all addresses in the \f[I]From:\f[R]
including duplicates, then sorts them and eliminates duplicates
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
jaro search to:nettime-l | jaro extract stdin from | sort | uniq
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Depending from the size of your nettime archives, this operation may
take some time and one may not want to repeat it in order to compute
some stats on the extract result.
So one can go on and send the old output to a new command:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
jaro replay extract | jaro stat names
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
This will print out statistics about the most prolific write to the
nettime list according to your archives.
.SS Statistics in brief
.PP
All \f[B]stats\f[R] commands takes lists of addresses or email messages
from stdin.
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
l l.
T{
command
T}@T{
effect
T}
_
T{
stats email
T}@T{
reads addresses from stdin, prints out stats on frequency of emails
found
T}
T{
stats names
T}@T{
reads addresses from stdin, prints out stats on frequency of names found
T}
T{
stats folders
T}@T{
reads paths to messages from stdin, prints out stats on frequency of
folders
T}
.TE
.PP
So in case of \f[B]stats email\f[R] or \f[B]stats names\f[R] any result
of search must be first filtered by \f[B]extract\f[R] in order to
provide addresses to stats, else errors will occur.
To limit the stats to the \f[I]From:\f[R] field use the \f[B]extract
stdin from\f[R] also shown in examples, any other refinement can be done
also in the domain of the search commands.
.SS Addressbook
.PP
Addressbooks are the files storing the whitelist, the blacklist and
optionally other custom lists of addresses.
The format we use is native \f[B]abook\f[R] database files, by
convention in \f[I]$JAROMAILDIR/whitelist.abook\f[R] and
\f[I]$JAROMAILDIR/blacklist.abook\f[R].
More custom addressbooks can be used by specifying them using
\f[B]-l\f[R] on the commandline, for instance \f[B]-l family\f[R] will
query the \f[I]$JAROMAILDIR/family.abook\f[R] addressbook; when not
used, \f[B]whitelist\f[R] is the default.
.PP
Addressbooks can be edited using a interactive console interface, for
instance to add or delete entries by hand: use the \f[B]abook\f[R]
command and optionally the \f[B]-l\f[R] option.
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
jaro abook
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
This will open the current whitelist for edit.
To edit the blacklist add \f[B]-l blacklist\f[R] instead.
.PP
To quickly dump to the console all names and addresses in the Jaro Mail
addressbook, one can use the \f[B]list\f[R] command
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
jaro list
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
To match a string across the addressbook, simply use the composite
command \f[B]addr\f[R] followed by strings, for instance:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
jaro addr dyne
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
will list all addresses containing \[aq]dyne\[aq] in your whitelist.
.SS Address lists
.PP
Jaro Mail handles lists of addresses as plain text files or streams with
entries formatted as \[aq]\f[I]Name <email>\f[R]\[aq] and newline
terminated.
This simple format conforms (or is normalized to) the RFC822 standard
and UTF-8 charset encoding, both produced on \f[I]stdout\f[R] and read
from \f[I]stdin\f[R] by various useful commands to take advantage of
console piping.
.PP
Such lists of addresses are the output of the \f[B]extract\f[R] command,
which is able to read the output of other commands and extract a list of
email addresses found.
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
jaro search open source date:2w..  | jaro extract stdin
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Will print to stdout the list of addresses found among the results of a
search for \f[I]open source\f[R] through all the emails archived in the
past 2 weeks.
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
jaro search date:1y.. and folder:known | jaro extract
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Will print a sorted list of unique addresses found in the emails
matching the search expression \[aq]\f[I]date:1y..
and folder:known\f[R]\[aq], meaning all messages stored in the
\[aq]\f[I]known\f[R]\[aq] folder and not older than 1 year from now.
.PP
The \f[B]import\f[R] command is complementary to extraction: it reads an
address list from stdin and imports it inside an addressbook specified
using \[aq]-l\[aq] or a \f[I]group\f[R] list file provided as argument.
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
jaro search folder:unsorted | jaro extract | jaro import -l blacklist
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Will extract all addresses found in unsorted (the maildir collecting all
non-mailinglist emails in which we are not an explicit recipient) and
put them into our blacklist.
.SS Private recipient groups
.PP
You can define reusable local recipient lists in \f[V]Mail/Groups/\f[R]
and send to them by using the pseudo-address
\f[V]groupname\[at]jaromail.group\f[R].
.PP
Create a group file, for example \f[V]Mail/Groups/team\f[R], with one
recipient per line:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
#mode individual
Alice <alice\[at]example.org>
Bob <bob\[at]example.org>
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Blank lines and comments are ignored.
Recipients may be written either as bare addresses or as display names
with addresses in angle brackets.
Duplicate addresses are removed case-insensitively.
If any non-comment line does not contain a valid email address, the
message is not queued and is moved to \f[V]postponed\f[R].
.PP
Then compose to the group:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
jaro compose team\[at]jaromail.group
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Group delivery modes are controlled by the first line:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]#mode individual\f[R] (default): one message per recipient,
recipients do not see each other.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]#mode carboncopy\f[R] or \f[V]#mode cc\f[R]: one message with all
recipients in \f[V]To:\f[R] and \f[V]Reply-To:\f[R] set to sender
address.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]#mode bcc\f[R]: one message with hidden recipients
(\f[V]To:   undisclosed-recipients:;\f[R]) and all recipients in
\f[V]Bcc:\f[R].
.PP
This behavior is implemented in the queueing path, so it also applies
when messages are piped and queued from stdin.
.SS Export to VCard and other formats
.PP
VCard is an exchange format useful to interface with other addressbook
software and mobile phones, as well with spyware as Google and Apple
mail.
Jaro Mail supports converting address lists to a variety of formats
thanks to \f[I]abook\f[R]:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
jaro addr | jaro export vcard
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Will take the list of addresses in whitelist and convert it to the
\f[B]vcard\f[R] format on stdout, ready to be redirected to a file.
.PP
Here below a list of output formats supported as argument to export:
